WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.093
The beating heart of a zebrafish embryo can be watched,

00:00:04.093 --> 00:00:07.007
but measurement demands more than watching. The camera

00:00:07.007 --> 00:00:09.557
must catch events faster than the eye.

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:22.507
Inside a developing zebrafish, the heart contracts and

00:00:22.507 --> 00:00:26.057
blood circulates across structures small enough to

00:00:26.057 --> 00:00:27.579
challenge ordinary observation.

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:37.348
Fast video microscopy captures change over time. The

00:00:37.348 --> 00:00:40.758
task becomes extracting meaningful cardiac and flow

00:00:40.758 --> 00:00:42.707
measurements from those frames.

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:51.092
The published work examines cardiac function and

00:00:51.092 --> 00:00:54.735
blood-flow hemodynamics, rather than treating a single

00:00:54.735 --> 00:00:57.337
attractive frame as quantitative physiology.

00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:05.864
Frame rate, optical scale and analysis assumptions can

00:01:05.864 --> 00:01:09.175
constrain an estimated velocity or physiological

00:01:09.175 --> 00:01:09.727
parameter.

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:19.168
A frame sequence becomes a flow estimate only after

00:01:19.168 --> 00:01:23.298
timing and image analysis are explicit. Do not show a

00:01:23.298 --> 00:01:26.190
simulated velocity field as a measured one.

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:35.626
Vessels change shape while blood moves. Interpretation

00:01:35.626 --> 00:01:39.708
has to respect both the organism and the measurement

00:01:39.708 --> 00:01:40.162
technique.

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:49.709
High-speed microscopy is valuable because movement

00:01:49.709 --> 00:01:52.968
becomes quantifiable, with its uncertainties visible

00:01:52.968 --> 00:01:54.597
rather than hidden.
